if len(adv):
 top=trait_table.iloc[0]
 para(report,f'The largest overrepresentation among prespecified binary traits was {top.trait}: {pct(top.adversarial_pct)} in the subset versus {pct(top.test_pct)} overall, a {fmt(top.difference_pp,2)}-point difference. Potential mechanisms include omitted interaction terms, model misspecification, and unobserved information. These are hypotheses, not verified explanations.')
else:para(report,'There were no confident errors at the stated cutoffs. The notebook follows the instruction to use all available rows, even if zero.')
report.add_heading('Part 2.4 — Fairness threshold trade-offs',1)
para(report,'Group A = Female; Group B = Male. DP = selection-rate ratio A/B; EO = min(TPR_A/TPR_B, FPR_A/FPR_B); PP = positive predictive value ratio A/B, following the exact assignment definitions. Undefined ratios have been left undefined rather than replaced. These are directional ratios: 1 is ideal, but values above 1 also indicate disparity.')
add_table(report,fairness[['threshold','DP','EO','PP','accuracy']])
report.add_picture(str(OUT/'fairness_tradeoff.png'),width=Inches(5.9))
if tradeoff:
 gain,loss,t0,t1,dp0,dp1,eo0,eo1=tradeoff
 para(report,f'An observed threshold comparison moves from t={t0:.2f} (DP={fmt(dp0)}, EO={fmt(eo0)}) to t={t1:.2f} (DP={fmt(dp1)}, EO={fmt(eo1)}). Distance of DP to 1 improves by {fmt(gain,4)} while distance of EO to 1 worsens by {fmt(loss,4)}; absolute-gap cost is {fmt(loss/gain,3)}. The brief’s percentage cost cannot be meaningfully equated with distance-to-ideal without defining a denominator.')
else:para(report,'No threshold pair in this experiment simultaneously improved DP’s distance to one and worsened EO’s distance to one. A universal trade-off is not demonstrated by these particular empirical results.')
para(report,f'The minimax disparity rule, with accuracy tie-breaking, selects t={chosen.threshold:.2f}: DP={fmt(chosen.DP)}, EO={fmt(chosen.EO)}, PP={fmt(chosen.PP)}, accuracy={fmt(chosen.accuracy)}. This selection rule is a value judgment and should not be mistaken for a uniquely optimal policy threshold.')
report.add_heading('Part 3 — Descriptive responses',1)
responses=[
('1. Why classifiers disagree',f'In the 300-person held-out set, the OLS-threshold and logistic 0.5 decisions disagreed for {mismatch} people ({pct(mismatch_pct)}). The linear score threshold was its observed median {fmt(med,5)}, rather than the logistic probability cutoff 0.50. OLS is a best-fit linear estimate of a binary indicator; predictions are not constrained to [0,1], nor necessarily calibrated probabilities. The logistic model fits log-odds and maps them through a sigmoid, then applies 0.5. Even where the same features were used, coefficient estimates and their response curves differ. A relative median cut separates an approximately equal number of test cases, while an absolute 0.5 cutoff reflects predicted class probability. The disagreement count provides evidence that a threshold is part of the decision policy, not a mere presentation setting. The confusion matrix above shows the direction of this disagreement. Before deployment, one would also assess probability calibration, subgroup false approvals/rejections, and whether the chosen threshold reflects actual costs and available credit capacity. The assignment uses high risk as a label, but our target is “good credit”; thus we consistently interpret positive prediction as good-credit/approval proxy rather than a high-risk event.'),
('2. Subgroup comparison',f'The exploratory best-differentiating predictor was {selected}. Its pooled coefficient was {fmt(subgroup_table.iloc[0].coef,5)}, male coefficient {fmt(subgroup_table.iloc[1].coef,5)}, and female coefficient {fmt(subgroup_table.iloc[2].coef,5)}. The 95% Wald confidence intervals were [{fmt(subgroup_table.iloc[0].lower_95,4)}, {fmt(subgroup_table.iloc[0].upper_95,4)}] pooled; [{fmt(subgroup_table.iloc[1].lower_95,4)}, {fmt(subgroup_table.iloc[1].upper_95,4)}] male; [{fmt(subgroup_table.iloc[2].lower_95,4)}, {fmt(subgroup_table.iloc[2].upper_95,4)}] female. An exploratory difference p-value was {fmt(comp.iloc[0].difference_p,5)}. If signs differ, this is descriptive evidence of subgroup reversal; otherwise the magnitude comparison is the relevant finding, and a non-significant difference must not be characterized as statistically established. Personal-status coding is an imperfect proxy for sex, and conditional coefficients may shift because groups differ on correlated variables. Ignoring this split could motivate a uniform rule even where the same predictor has different error implications. A policy response should involve independent subgroup validation, measurement reviews, and a discussion of whether group-aware interventions would themselves be lawful and ethical.'),
('3. Omitted variable',f'The removed variable was duration. The table in Part 2.2 gives full and reduced coefficients for credit amount, installment rate, age, and existing credits alongside their signed percentage changes. Of those, {strongest.predictor} moved the most in relative absolute value ({pct(strongest.pct_change)}), from {fmt(strongest.full_coef,4)} to {fmt(strongest.reduced_coef,4)}. Loan duration plausibly co-varies with loan size and repayment burden, so excluding it changes the associations that the remaining coefficients absorb. However, coefficient changes are not automatically causal evidence: omitted-variable bias, regularization differences, collinearity, and logistic-model non-collapsibility are distinct concerns. Here the likelihood specification and training matrix are fixed except for the deletion, making the comparison operationally meaningful. A safer practice is to maintain a causal assumptions register, audit data provenance, and test alternative feature sets and datasets. Confidence in a predictive model should depend on whether conclusions are robust across reasonable specifications, not on the appearance of a stable global accuracy.'),
('4. Detecting a hidden confound',f'A practical detection plan would start with an independent lender or credit bureau dataset collected in a different period, and verify label definitions, selection criteria, and the availability of income, debt-to-income ratio, and repayment history. The current model has {len(Xtr)} training and {len(Xte)} test observations and uses {len(feature_names)} encoded predictors; its test AUC was {fmt(roc_auc_score(yte,prob))}. First, ask underwriting experts to draw a directed causal graph, listing plausible upstream causes of both each predictor and actual default. Next, externally validate overall and conditional calibration across credit amount, duration, age and sex-coded groups, comparing residual error patterns to this internal test. Then refit with newly obtained confound candidates and measure coefficient changes against the omitted-duration exercise, in which the largest observed relative movement was {pct(strongest.pct_change)}. Document which variables are missing due to collection limits, not simply missing values. Repeated correlations across datasets cannot prove absence of unmeasured confounding, but systematic changes can reveal fragility. Require a prospective monitored pilot and human appeals process rather than extrapolating training-set associations directly into lending decisions.'),
('5. Adversarial cases',f'The model made {adv_count} high-confidence errors, representing {pct(adv_pct)} of the 300-case held-out set; {len(adv)} were sampled when available. A positive confident mistake predicts approval probability at least 0.75 for an observed bad-credit record; a negative mistake predicts at most 0.25 for an observed good-credit record. The sample median age was {fmt(adv.age.median()) if len(adv) else "undefined"} versus {fmt(test_df.age.median())} in the held-out population. '+(f'The most overrepresented measured characteristic was {top.trait} ({pct(top.adversarial_pct)} versus {pct(top.test_pct)} in the full test set).' if len(adv) else 'There were no qualifying confident mistakes, so no stable subset trait can be inferred.')+' The linear logistic score sums feature effects; it lacks explicit interactions or non-linearities beyond those embedded in encoding. A confident error may occur when different feature combinations share the same sum but have different true outcomes. This is a plausible structural mechanism rather than a demonstrated causal reason. Investigate calibration, model interactions, missing factors, temporal drift and statistical uncertainty before describing affected applicants as anomalous.'),
('6. Human consequences of fairness metrics',f'Consider two hypothetical applicants who differ in sex-coded group but have otherwise similar credit histories. At t=0.05 and t=0.95 the corresponding observed selection ratios DP are {fmt(fairness.iloc[0].DP)} and {fmt(fairness.iloc[-1].DP)}, and EO ratios are {fmt(fairness.iloc[0].EO)} and {fmt(fairness.iloc[-1].EO)}. A person given probability 0.40 receives an approval-proxy positive decision at threshold 0.35 but not at 0.45; this is a mathematical illustration rather than an assertion that such individuals occur in the held-out data. Choosing a lenient cutoff can increase approvals but can also increase false approvals differently across groups; a severe cutoff can deny credit to otherwise creditworthy individuals. Demographic parity measures selection balance, while equalized odds concerns error rates conditional on true outcome. The effect on people cannot be inferred from ratios alone: stakeholders must determine the relative harms of excluding a good applicant, lending irresponsibly, and using a sex-derived field in the decision process. No single ratio resolves those competing welfare considerations.'),
('7. Defending a threshold',f'An explicit minimax-disparity criterion recommends t={chosen.threshold:.2f}, where DP={fmt(chosen.DP)}, EO={fmt(chosen.EO)}, PP={fmt(chosen.PP)}, and held-out accuracy={fmt(chosen.accuracy)}. The selection criterion minimizes the largest absolute departure of the three reported directional ratios from 1, breaking ties in favor of higher accuracy. It is defensible as a transparent attempt not to maximize one fairness notion at the complete expense of others. Its principal weakness is that ratios are noisy with only 300 test observations and may be undefined at extremes. In addition, a directional ratio of 1 can obscure differences in sample size or absolute base rates. A critic could argue that treating parity and equalized odds as equally important is unjustified for credit allocation, where rejecting deserving borrowers may be more harmful than other errors. This objection is valid: threshold design needs an explicit stakeholder cost model, confidence intervals, legal assessment and external replication. The numeric result is an illustration of the objective, not authorization for automatic deployment.'),
('8. Parity versus odds',f'For this dataset I would give greater weight to equalized odds than demographic parity, provided reliability is validated across groups. Equalized odds distinguishes true approvals of good-credit cases from erroneous approvals of bad-credit cases; demographic parity only asks whether predicted approvals occur at the same overall rate. At the candidate threshold {chosen.threshold:.2f}, the observed DP ratio is {fmt(chosen.DP)} and EO ratio is {fmt(chosen.EO)}; predictive parity is {fmt(chosen.PP)}. If base rates differ for historical, institutional or discriminatory reasons, rigid demographic parity can conflict with other metrics. Conversely, privileging error-rate balance may preserve historically unequal approval rates, potentially reinforcing existing structural disadvantage. That sacrifice is not automatically acceptable: the underlying “good/bad credit” labels may themselves contain past bias. My preference therefore applies only within a controlled pilot with transparent appeal routes, group-level uncertainty estimates and review of how outcome labels were generated. A single threshold is not a moral determination; it is a policy choice conditioned on uncertain data and differing harms.')]
for title,body in responses:
 report.add_heading(title,2);para(report,body)
report.add_heading('Part 4 — Reflection: should this model be deployed?',1)
reflection=f"""I recommend conditional non-deployment for automated credit decisions: the model should remain in research or a carefully governed shadow evaluation until its fairness, data validity and harm profile can be independently validated. The headline reason is that a credible aggregate predictive number is not sufficient evidence of fair decisions.

First, the very definition of a good decision depends on a threshold. In this experiment, the OLS model thresholded at the median predicted outcome ({fmt(med,5)}) disagreed with a logistic model thresholded at 0.50 for {mismatch} of {len(yte)} people ({pct(mismatch_pct)}). Neither threshold is simply “what the data says.” The median creates a relative split, whereas the logistic threshold makes an absolute probability-based classification. In credit, changing the threshold changes who may be denied funding or offered risky loans. A policymaker should ask whose welfare is protected and whose losses are counted, instead of blindly maximizing one summary measure.

Second, group disparities remain even when a global model appears useful. The logistic classifier achieved {fmt(accuracy_score(yte,flag_B))} accuracy and {fmt(roc_auc_score(yte,prob))} AUC, yet the candidate fairness threshold {chosen.threshold:.2f} produced demographic parity {fmt(chosen.DP)}, equalized odds {fmt(chosen.EO)} and predictive parity {fmt(chosen.PP)}. These are distinct conditional questions. Our directional ratios can be undefined for certain thresholds and may be unstable when group counts shrink. Those limitations are not bugs to hide; they are reasons to disclose denominators and uncertainty. More importantly, the historical good/bad credit target is not a known bank approval decision and can reproduce previous exclusion and measurement errors.

Third, the fitted relationships are specification-sensitive. Removing duration changed the {strongest.predictor} coefficient from {fmt(strongest.full_coef,4)} to {fmt(strongest.reduced_coef,4)}, a signed relative change of {pct(strongest.pct_change)}. This does not prove a causal confound, but it shows how the story attached to a model can change with feature choices. The subgroup analysis likewise found an exploratory difference for {selected}. Unobserved income, lending policy, selection effects and temporal drift could produce further changes not detectable from this one dataset. A model may be operationally reproducible yet scientifically incomplete.

Fourth, the classifier produced {adv_count} confident wrong predictions, {pct(adv_pct)} of the held-out set. Such mistakes are especially consequential because confident scores can make reviewers less willing to override automated recommendations. Any rollout would need subgroup-conditioned calibration audits, human review, explanations that show uncertainty, mechanisms to correct records and evidence from a different period or lender.

The numbers above argue against a one-number deployment decision, not against modelling altogether. Responsible use requires a documented decision objective, alternatives to automation, valid labels, a legal and ethical assessment of protected attributes, external evaluation, an appeals process and ongoing drift surveillance. The meta-lesson is that transparency about trade-offs is a prerequisite for responsible AI: a single accuracy value cannot encode society’s competing values."""
para(report,reflection)
report.add_heading('Reproducibility and references',1)
para(report,'All estimates, exact tables and chart files are created from the accompanying Colab notebook using seed 843. The original dataset is Statlog (German Credit Data), UCI Machine Learning Repository: https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/144/statlog+german+credit+data . The data producer notes that the cost of classifying a bad customer as good is different from the opposite error; this report does not assume equal business harms. The subgroup comparisons are exploratory, and this report is a methodological classroom exercise, not a lending tool.')
